<Base title="Methodology">
  <div class="study">
    <p class="eyebrow">How this research works</p><h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="lead">A Jesus-centered question with a transparent, non-predetermined comparison method.</p>
    <section class="panel"><h2>Four layers of interpretation</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Text:</strong> what a passage says in its immediate and larger literary context.</li>
        <li><strong>History:</strong> what its author and audience may plausibly have understood.</li>
        <li><strong>Theology:</strong> how competing traditions interpret the claim.</li>
        <li><strong>Application:</strong> what can reasonably be inferred for modern issues.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>
    <h2>Separate the witnesses</h2>
    <p>Gospel portrayals of Jesus, seven generally undisputed letters of Paul, six debated letters, Acts, and the remaining New Testament books are analyzed separately. Disputed authorship and manuscript issues are marked where relevant.</p>
    <h2>Test both sides</h2>
    <p>Comparisons can show agreement, different emphasis, extension, apparent tension, direct contradiction, or insufficient evidence. Each substantive claim requires source checks and credible counterreadings.</p>
    <h2>Research status</h2>
    <p><span class="status" data-state="draft">draft</span> a preliminary outline; <span class="status">source-checked</span> checked against sources; <span class="status">reviewed</span> independently reviewed.</p>
    <p>The full working methodology is maintained at <a href="https://github.com/benjaminarthurt/Jesus-paul-study/blob/main/docs/methodology.md">docs/methodology.md</a> in the private project repository. Access requires repository permission.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
